// Source: vendor/apelin/Packages/ApelinAgent/Sources/ApelinAgent/Skills/BuiltIn/PackageRepository.swift
extension MinisApelinBuiltInSkills {
    static let packageRepository = MinisApelinSkillDocument(
        name: "apelin-package-repository",
        description: "Maintaining the user's package repository (an APT source on GitHub Pages): its layout, the packages in debs/, depictions, the control fields a package needs, how a release is published, and keeping old versions in check.",
        body: """
        # Maintaining a package repository

        A package repository is an APT source the user's devices and other
        people add in Irisin. Apelin keeps one in a GitHub
        repository of the user's, set up from Settings ▸ Git ▸ Publishing:
        Create Distribution Repository makes it from Apelin's template, and
        Link Distribution Repository takes one the user has, adding what the
        template has and it lacks. Either way its folder is a workspace
        project, and that project's conversation maintains it with the file
        and git tools. GitHub Pages serves it at
        `https://<owner>.github.io/<repository>/`, the address users add as a
        source.

        ## Layout
        - `debs/`: the packages, one `.deb` each, named
          `<Package>_<Version>_<Architecture>.deb`. No two files have the same
          name, nor the same Package, Version and Architecture.
        - `depictions/<package id>/`: optional, per package: `index.html` (the
          web depiction), `depiction.json` (the native depiction Irisin shows),
          `icon.png` and `banner.png`. Pages serves them at
          `<source>/depictions/<package id>/…`, the addresses a package's
          `Depiction`, `SileoDepiction` and `Icon` fields name: in a project,
          `package.depiction`, `package.sileoDepiction` and `package.icon`
          (the apelin-package-depiction skill).
        - `index.html`: the source's home page; `CydiaIcon.png`: its icon.
        - `sileo-featured.json`: the banners Irisin shows at the top of the
          source, each naming a package and an image under `depictions/`.
        - `scripts/update-repository.sh` and
          `.github/workflows/update-repository.yml`: on every push to `main`,
          GitHub Actions indexes `debs/` with apt-ftparchive into `Packages`,
          `Packages.xz` and `Release` and publishes the site to Pages. Those
          indexes and `_site/` are built there; never commit them.

        ## Publishing a release
        - A project's Distribute… page builds a release package for each
          bootstrap (`iphoneos-arm64e` for roothide, `iphoneos-arm64` for
          rootless), and its Publish copies the package into `debs/` here,
          commits and pushes. The source shows it once the workflow has run,
          usually within a few minutes; Pages may serve the old index for up
          to ten more.
        - From a conversation, `apelin_package_publish` does the same with
          release builds already made: the roothide and the rootless build
          of one version in one call, each exactly as its build made it.
          Call it only when the user asked to publish them, or agreed when
          you asked. Never copy a `.deb` into `debs/` yourself: the tool
          holds each package to the digest its build recorded.
        - A package's control file needs `Package`, `Name`, `Version`,
          `Architecture`, `Description`, `Maintainer`, `Author` and `Section`
          (the package's category: Tweaks, Applications, Utilities, Themes…); the
          workflow fails without `Section`. A project sets them in its
          apelin-project.json (the apelin-project-description skill), not here.
        - A new version is a new file. Never replace a published file under
          the same name: clients compare it with the index they cached.

        ## Maintaining it
        - Keep the newest few versions of each package. Remove an older one
          with shell_run (`rm debs/<file>`), then `git_commit` with `all: true`
          and `git_push`.
        - Write depictions and featured banners when the user asks for a
          better page; keep images small (a banner around 1000×400, an icon
          120×120, PNG).
        - The workflow's own failures show only on GitHub, in the repository's
          Actions tab: when the source does not update, say so and point the
          user there; you cannot read them from here.
        - Pushing publishes: in Strict mode `git_push` asks the user, and that
          is the step that makes a change public.
        """,
    )
}

// Source: vendor/apelin/Packages/ApelinAgent/Sources/ApelinAgent/Skills/BuiltIn/PackagingAndInstall.swift
extension MinisApelinBuiltInSkills {
    static let packagingAndInstall = MinisApelinSkillDocument(
        name: "apelin-packaging-and-install",
        description: "From a successful build to a confirmed load: the package artifact, the package and bundle identifiers to settle before the first install and how to change one after it, install through the system installer and what to do when another package is in its way, maintainer scripts, respring, apelin_package_confirm_loaded, and removal.",
        body: """
        # Packaging and install

        `apelin_project_build` compiles, links, makes the dSYM, strips and signs (signing is
        last; nothing touches the binary after it), then writes the `.deb`. Its
        result lists artifacts with an `id`, a `kind` and a `digest`.

        ## Where the package goes
        - The `.deb` stays in Apelin's folder for that build, outside the
          project; the file tools do not reach it. It is the project's only
          package: never repack, rename or copy it into the project, and
          never make one yourself with `dpkg-deb -b`, `ar` or `tar`, to test
          or to hand over. A workspace's declared build script is the one
          place a package is put together (apelin-external-build).
        - A user who wants the package file takes it from Distribute… in the
          project chat's More (…) menu: it runs a release build for the
          bootstrap they choose, then exports that package. Release builds
          you already made go to the user's package repository with
          `apelin_package_publish`, as they are, when the user asks for it
          (apelin-package-repository).

        ## Package identity
        - A new project's `package.id` in apelin-project.json, and an app's
          `CFBundleIdentifier` in its Info.plist, start as `local.apelin.…`
          placeholders Apelin made up from the folder name. Before the first
          install, ask the user for permanent reverse-DNS identifiers, or
          whether to keep these, and write each one everywhere the project
          spells it: `file_grep` for `local.apelin.` finds each place, such
          as an app's `application-identifier` entitlement and a preference
          bundle's `Root.plist` domain. `apelin_project_create` takes them
          up front as `packageID` and `bundleID` in `values`.
        - Debug and release builds carry the same identifiers: a release is
          the same package at a higher version, never a renamed one.
        - `apelin_package_list` with this project says whether a package of
          it is installed. Once one is, its identifiers are established:
          never change them on your own, and never assume a build under a
          new package ID upgrades the installed package in place. The new ID
          is another package, and the old one stays installed until it is
          removed. When the user wants one changed, agree the migration with
          them before installing:
          - A new package that installs a file the old one installed (the
            same dylib or app bundle) is refused, naming the old package in
            `conflicts` (below); one that installs none goes in beside it,
            and both stay installed. Either way the plan is to remove the old
            installation with `apelin_package_remove` (in Strict mode it
            asks), then install the new package, and only with the user's
            agreement.
          - Nothing follows the identity: an app's data and preferences, a
            preferences domain named for the package ID (the preference
            bundle template's) and anything else keyed by the old bundle ID
            or package ID stay where they were. Say so, and ask whether any
            of it must be carried over before the old package goes.

        ## Install
        - Call `apelin_package_install` with the `package` artifact's `id` and `digest`
          exactly as `apelin_project_build` returned them. The host copies that artifact to
          private staging and checks the digest before the installer sees it; a
          mismatch is refused, so never guess or reuse a digest from an older
          build.
        - The packaging description (control fields, maintainer scripts,
          install layout, dependencies) is compared with the template. Matching
          installs run; a changed one asks the user once, showing the package
          id, the diff and every maintainer script. Do not add maintainer scripts
          unless the task needs them; they run as root.
        - The result carries a `transactionID` and, on success, an
          `installationID`, which `apelin_package_remove` takes;
          `apelin_package_list` finds it again later.
        - Installation goes through Irisin's installer. There is no `dpkg` or
          `apt` for you to call, and `shell_run` must not try; the
          apelin-package-dependencies skill covers what installs a dependency.

        ## When another package is in the way
        The installer resolves nothing: it never removes a package to make
        room for yours. An install fails when an installed package owns one
        of its files (`… is owned by …; Replaces is required`) or when its
        `package.conflicts` or `package.breaks` names an installed package
        (`Conflicting final packages`). The result's `conflicts` then names
        each package in the way, the file, and the `installationID` when
        Apelin installed it.
        - After a package ID change the user agreed to (Package identity,
          above), or reusing a product name another package already
          installs, remove the installation named there with
          `apelin_package_remove` (in Strict mode the user confirms it), then
          install again. A package Apelin did not install is the
          user's to remove with their package manager: ask them.
        - Adding `conflicts` or `breaks` removes nothing; it only makes the
          install fail while that package is there.
        - Name a package in `package.replaces` only when the user wants
          yours to take over its files.
        - Never rename the dylib or drop a file to get past the failure
          without telling the user.

        ## Maintainer scripts
        Add one only when the task needs it (postinst, prerm, …), as a project
        file named in `package.maintainerScripts`. They run as **root**, in
        the bootstrap, and the install card shows each in full:
        - Spell paths `@PREFIX@/…` (bootstrap) and `@ROOTFS@/…` (system), the
          only tokens substituted, as the apelin-bootstrap-paths skill says; `sh -n`
          must pass.
        - Start with `set -e`. A path a script removes, moves or overwrites
          is written out in full (`@PREFIX@/Library/…`); one that has a
          variable in it spells it `${name:?}`, so the script stops when the
          variable is empty instead of running `rm -rf` on `/` as root.
        - A daemon your package ships is loaded by its postinst: boot the
          label out of `system/`, `user/501/` and `gui/501/` (roothide's
          launchctl can put a bootstrap daemon in the per-user domain), then
          `launchctl bootstrap system "@PREFIX@/Library/LaunchDaemons/<label>.plist"`;
          prerm boots it out on `remove`. Keep the label in a variable alone
          on its line. A daemon your tweak injects into is restarted by your
          postinst too; a SpringBoard tweak needs a respring, not a script.
        - Do not register the app, rebuild the icon cache or respring from a
          script, and do not `mkdir -p` a folder for mobile as root (it leaves
          the parents root-owned): make each level with `mkdir -m 0755` and
          `chown -h 501:501`, skipping a level that exists and never following
          a symlink.
        - A link you ship or create is relative (apelin-bootstrap-paths).

        ## Respring and confirm
        - A tweak loaded into SpringBoard needs `apelin_device_respring`; one
          loaded into an app takes effect when that app starts again. After
          respring the session continues; the app may reconnect.
        - An app project: installing replaces the bundle on disk, but a copy
          already running keeps the previous build until it exits, and the
          install does not stop it (its result's `relaunchHint` says so).
          Restart it through shell_run as mobile, never with `root: true`:
          `{"argv": ["icli", "app", "kill", "--force", "<bundle id>"]}` (icli
          refuses a kill without `--force`), then
          `{"argv": ["icli", "app", "launch", "<bundle id>"]}`. The kill
          answers the pids it stopped; before saying the new build runs,
          check that `icli app running` lists the app under another pid. The
          bundle id is the `CFBundleIdentifier` of the app product's
          Info.plist; `icli app list` names it too.
        - `apelin_package_confirm_loaded` with the `buildID` and the target process reports
          the marker log line and whether the filter selects the target,
          separately; its `loaded` rests on the marker alone, since the image
          list is never read, on any iOS version. The marker is logged once,
          when the target starts, and is only seen while `apelin_package_confirm_loaded`
          listens (15 s; 30 s with a respring): for a SpringBoard tweak pass
          `respring: true` instead of a separate `apelin_device_respring`. For an app, your
          tool calls run one at a time, so quit it first if it runs
          (`icli app kill --force <bundle id>`), then ask the user to open it
          while the call listens. "Installed" is not "loaded": report both.
        - If nothing loaded: check the filter plist's bundle id against the
          target, the injection framework dependency (`mobilesubstrate` in
          `depends`), then `apelin_log_read` for the target process. A daemon
          started with `DISABLE_TWEAKS=1`, and an app on roothide's blacklist,
          load no tweaks at all.

        ## Removal
        - `apelin_package_list` lists what Apelin installed that is still on
          the device, from every project and conversation: per package, the
          installation id, version, the project whose build it was, and
          whether it can be removed. Read it instead of rebuilding or
          reinstalling to get an id, and instead of asking another project's
          conversation to remove its package.
        - `apelin_package_remove` takes that installation id, one an install
          returned or a refused install names in `conflicts`, or the package
          id alone, and removes only what a project's build installed:
          bootstrap packages, Apelin itself and its toolchains (removed in
          Settings) are out of reach, and a package installed some other way
          is the user's to remove with their package manager. In Strict mode
          every removal asks.
        """,
    )
}

// Source: vendor/apelin/Packages/ApelinAgent/Sources/ApelinAgent/Skills/BuiltIn/PreferenceBundle.swift
extension MinisApelinBuiltInSkills {
    static let preferenceBundle = MinisApelinSkillDocument(
        name: "apelin-preference-bundle",
        description: "A Settings pane for a tweak: the PSListController subclass, Root.plist specifiers, the PreferenceLoader entry, the preferences domain it stores to, and how the tweak reads the same setting.",
        body: """
        # A preference bundle

        A tweak's settings live in a preference bundle that PreferenceLoader
        shows inside the Settings app. Start from the Objective-C Preference
        Bundle template (`kind: preferences`); it is the shape below.

        ## The pieces
        - **The principal class** is a `PSListController` subclass (its name
          is the bundle's `NSPrincipalClass`). It loads its rows from
          `Root.plist` and lets the framework read and write the values:
          ```objc
          #import <Preferences/PSListController.h>
          @interface MyRootListController : PSListController
          @end
          @implementation MyRootListController
          - (NSArray *)specifiers {
              if (_specifiers == nil) {
                  _specifiers = [self loadSpecifiersFromPlistName:@"Root" target:self];
              }
              return _specifiers;
          }
          @end
          ```
          Cache in the framework's own `_specifiers` ivar, not a static, so a
          second showing reloads. Let `PSListController` read and write
          values through `readPreferenceValue:`/`setPreferenceValue:`; do not
          override them with NSUserDefaults. The `Preferences` framework is
          private: it is reached through the preferences extension's support
          component (apelin-project-description), which supplies the declarations and
          a link stub — do not expect SDK headers for it.
        - **The declarations** are one header,
          `<install root>/usr/share/apelin/development/wiki.qaq.apelin.dev.preferences/support/include/Preferences/PSListController.h`
          (the install root # Environment names), which
          `#import <Preferences/PSListController.h>` finds. Read it with
          file_read before calling the framework from code: it is what the
          compiler knows of `PSListController` and `PSSpecifier`. A method
          it lacks is declared in a category in the project's own source;
          never edit the support folder.
        - **Root.plist** is `items`, an array of specifiers, each a `cell`
          plus its keys:
          - `PSGroupCell` — a section header with `label` and optional
            `footerText`.
          - `PSSwitchCell` — a bool: `label`, `key`, `default`, `defaults`
            (the preferences domain), `PostNotification`.
          - `PSEditTextCell` — a string: the same keys.
          Other cells exist (`PSSliderCell`, `PSLinkListCell` with its value
          list, `PSButtonCell`, `PSLinkCell` for a child pane). `defaults` on
          a row is the domain the value is stored in — the package id — and
          `PostNotification` is the Darwin name posted after a change.
        - **The PreferenceLoader entry** is a plist at
          `/Library/PreferenceLoader/Preferences/<Name>.plist` with one
          `entry` dictionary: `bundle` (the bundle's name), `detail` (the
          controller class), `cell` `PSLinkCell`, `isController` true,
          `label` (the Settings row) and `icon` (the row's image, a file
          name in the bundle). The package depends on `preferenceloader`.
        - **The row's icon** is `icon.png` with `icon@2x.png` and
          `icon@3x.png`, 29 points (29, 58 and 87 pixels), a rounded square
          on a clear ground, listed in the product's `resources` so the
          bundle carries them. The template ships Apelin's preferences
          plate; replace the three files, same names and sizes, to give the
          pane its own. Without `icon` the row shows no image.
        - **The bundle Info.plist** names `NSPrincipalClass` and
          `CFBundlePackageType` `BNDL`.

        ## How the tweak reads it
        The pane writes the value into the package's preferences domain and
        posts `<package id>/changed`. The tweak reads that domain and listens
        for that name, as the apelin-tweak-runtime skill describes. Keep the row's
        `defaults`, the tweak's domain and the notification name the same
        string — the package id — on both sides.

        ## Layout
        - The bundle: `/Library/PreferenceBundles/<Name>.bundle` (a `bundle`
          product, with a `bundle-resources` step and `resources`:
          `Resources/Root.plist` and the three icon files).
        - The entry: `/Library/PreferenceLoader/Preferences/<Name>.plist`, a
          `{path, file}` layout entry, mode `0644`.
        Settings reads the row at launch; a change to the entry or the bundle
        shows after Settings is relaunched.
        """,
    )
}

// Source: vendor/apelin/Packages/ApelinAgent/Sources/ApelinAgent/Skills/BuiltIn/ProjectDescription.swift

extension MinisApelinBuiltInSkills {
    static let projectDescription = MinisApelinSkillDocument(
        name: "apelin-project-description",
        description: "Every member of apelin-project.json, the build description Apelin's build reads: products, flags, steps, package, layout, tweak filter, the macros the build defines, and what asks the user.",
        body: """
        # The project description

        `apelin-project.json` at the project root is the one thing Apelin's
        build reads. It is checked strictly: an unknown member, a duplicate
        key or a wrong type is an error naming its field, such as
        `/products/0/sources`. The complete schema is this skill's file
        `apelin-project.schema.json` (apelin_skill_read with that file).
        Apelin writes it two spaces a level, one member or element a line,
        and every build puts it back in that form, values unchanged: keep to
        it, and read the file again before file_edit after a build.

        ## Members Apelin writes
        Never invent, copy or change these:
        - `schemaVersion` (1) and `projectID`, a lowercase UUID approvals are
          bound to.
        - `template`: the template the project was made from.
        - `environment` `{toolchain, sdk}`, each `{id, digest}`: the
          registered toolchain and SDK. Change them only with
          apelin_project_set_environment; never type a digest.
        - `displayName` and `autoName`: the name Apelin shows.

        Only apelin_project_create writes `template` and `environment`, so a
        description written from scratch lacks them: it does not build, and
        no toolchain or SDK can be bound to it. Never rewrite a workspace
        (`"kind": "workspace"`) as one: it builds with a script of its own
        (the apelin-external-build skill).

        ## What it builds
        - `kind`: `tweak` (a `dylib` product and `package.tweakFilter`),
          `app` (an `app` product), `cli` (an `executable`) or `preferences`
          (a `bundle`). A project may carry more products than its kind's,
          such as a tweak with its own preference bundle.
        - `language`: `objc` and `c` compile `.m` and `.c` files only;
          `swift` compiles `.swift` only. A product never mixes them.
        - `deploymentTarget`: a string, "16.0" or later.
        - `architectures`: `{"rootless": ["arm64", "arm64e"], "roothide":
          ["arm64e"]}`. The keys present are the bootstraps it packages for.
        - `configurations`: `Debug` and `Release`, each `{optimization,
          debugInfo, defines?, cFlags?, swiftFlags?, linkFlags?}`.
          `optimization` is "0", "1", "2", "3", "s" or "z". `defines` maps a
          name to its value; no name starts with `APELIN_`. Each flag is one
          argument starting with `-`, never shell text: `-framework X` is two
          items. The member is `linkFlags`, not `linkerFlags`.

        ## products (one to eight)
        `{id, type, name, sources, …}`:
        - `id`: lowercase, such as `tweak`. `type`: `dylib`, `app`, `bundle`
          or `executable`. `name`: the Mach-O file name; product names are
          unique.
        - `sources`: project paths that may use `*`, `**` and `?`, such as
          `Sources/*.m` or `src/**/*.m`.
        - Optional: `headerSearchPaths`, `frameworks`, `weakFrameworks`,
          `libraries` (`-l` names, such as `substrate`),
          `frameworkSearchPaths` and `librarySearchPaths` (project paths),
          `objcARC`, `installName` (dylib only; default
          `@rpath/<name>.dylib`), `entitlements` (a project plist), and
          `infoPlist` and `resources` (app and bundle only).
        - `extensionSupport`: `[{extension, component}]`, the headers, link
          stubs and rpaths an installed development extension provides, the
          build picking each bootstrap's own. A tweak hooking with substrate
          has `{"extension": "wiki.qaq.apelin.dev.tweak", "component":
          "substrate"}`, `libraries: ["substrate"]` and depends on
          `mobilesubstrate`; a preference bundle has
          `{"extension": "wiki.qaq.apelin.dev.preferences", "component":
          "preferences"}`, `frameworks` with `Preferences`, and depends on
          `preferenceloader`.

        ## build.steps
        Per product, in this order: `compile`, `link`, `bundle-resources`
        (app and bundle), `lipo`, `dsym`, `strip`, `sign`. `compile`, `link`,
        `lipo` and `sign` are required and `sign` comes last, as `{"type":
        "sign", "product": "tweak", "identity": "adhoc"}`. `compile` and
        `link` take extra `flags`; `strip` takes `style`, `non-global` or
        `debug`. A `script` step, `{"type": "script", "id", "interpreter",
        "file", "arguments"?, "inputs"?, "outputs"?, "requiresApproval":
        true}`, runs a project script (sh, bash, zsh, python3 or perl) as
        mobile in the project root, to generate sources; it always asks the
        user in Strict mode.

        ## package
        `{id, name, version, description, author, section, layout, …}`:
        - `id`: the Debian package name; a new project's is a
          `local.apelin.` placeholder to settle with the user before its
          first install (the apelin-packaging-and-install skill's Package
          identity). `version`: a Debian version.
          `section`: Tweaks, Utilities, Development, System, Applications or
          Addons. Optional: `maintainer`, `longDescription` (a list of lines).
        - `icon`: the image a package manager shows beside the package, its
          control `Icon`: an `https://` URL in printable ASCII, at most 2048
          bytes. Every template writes its extension's icon on Apelin's
          Pages, such as
          `https://owngoal-dev.github.io/Apelin/icons/wiki.qaq.apelin.dev.tweak.png`.
          Keep it, or put in an https image the user gives as their own. It
          is not an app's own icon, which its Info.plist names.
        - `depiction` and `sileoDepiction`: optional, the package's page in a
          package manager, written as the control's `Depiction` (a web page)
          and `SileoDepiction` (the native depiction JSON, where a changelog
          tab comes from), each an `https://` URL of the icon's form. No
          template writes them; add one only with an address the user gave.
          The apelin-package-depiction skill has the JSON and where to host
          it.
        - Relations: `depends`, `recommends`, `suggests`, `conflicts`,
          `breaks`, `replaces` and `provides`, each item like `"name (>=
          1.0)"`, alternatives joined by ` | `; `dependsByBootstrap`
          `{rootless?, roothide?}` for one bootstrap only. The packager adds
          `firmware` and the `Architecture`; never write them.
        - `layout`: `{path, product}` or `{path, file, mode?,
          substitutePrefix?}` items. Paths are as the package sees them,
          rooted at the bootstrap, such as
          `/Library/MobileSubstrate/DynamicLibraries/X.dylib`; the packager
          maps them for rootless and roothide. Never write `/var/jb` or a
          jbroot path. `mode` is `0644` or `0755`; `substitutePrefix: true`
          fills `@PREFIX@` and `@ROOTFS@` in a file, such as a launch daemon
          plist (the apelin-bootstrap-paths skill).
        - `tweakFilter` (tweak only): `{dylib: <product id>, bundles?,
          executables?, classes?}`. The packager writes the filter plist
          beside the dylib, which must be laid out under
          `/Library/MobileSubstrate/DynamicLibraries/`; do not ship one
          yourself. A new tweak's `bundles` come from the template's
          `filterBundles`, an array of bundle IDs in apelin_project_create's
          `values`, such as `["com.apple.Preferences"]`.
        - `maintainerScripts`: `{preinst?, postinst?, prerm?, postrm?}`, each
          a project file. Only `@PREFIX@` and `@ROOTFS@` are substituted;
          `sh -n` must pass. They run as root and the install card shows
          them in full: add one only when the task needs it, written as the
          apelin-packaging-and-install skill says.

        ## What the build defines
        On every C and Objective-C compile: `APELIN_BUILD_ID`,
        `APELIN_PACKAGE_ID`, `APELIN_VERSION` and `APELIN_PRODUCT_NAME`
        (string literals); `APELIN_BOOTSTRAP_ROOTLESS` or
        `APELIN_BOOTSTRAP_ROOTHIDE` (1, for the bootstrap being built); and
        `APELIN_CONFIGURATION_DEBUG` or `APELIN_CONFIGURATION_RELEASE` (1).
        Swift gets the bootstrap and configuration as conditions. Code that
        behaves differently per bootstrap tests these; a repository's own
        macro is defined from them in a header. The build always passes the
        SDK, `-arch`, the deployment target, `-fvisibility=hidden` and
        `-Werror=unguarded-availability-new`, and `-fobjc-arc` with `objcARC`.

        ## What asks the user
        In Strict mode the next build asks once after a change to `kind`,
        `language`, `deploymentTarget`, `architectures`, `configurations`,
        `build`, `products` (except their `sources`, `headerSearchPaths` and
        `resources`), or an entitlements or script file. The next install
        asks once after a change to `package` (except `version`), a
        maintainer script or an installed file. Editing sources and bumping
        `version` never ask.

        ## A tweak's build members
        ```json
        "products": [{
          "id": "tweak", "type": "dylib", "name": "MyTweak",
          "sources": ["Sources/*.m"], "objcARC": true,
          "frameworks": ["Foundation", "UIKit"], "libraries": ["substrate"],
          "extensionSupport": [{"extension": "wiki.qaq.apelin.dev.tweak", "component": "substrate"}]
        }],
        "build": {"steps": [
          {"type": "compile", "product": "tweak"}, {"type": "link", "product": "tweak"},
          {"type": "lipo", "product": "tweak"}, {"type": "dsym", "product": "tweak"},
          {"type": "strip", "product": "tweak", "style": "non-global"},
          {"type": "sign", "product": "tweak", "identity": "adhoc"}
        ]},
        "package": {
          "id": "com.example.mytweak", "name": "MyTweak", "version": "1.0.0",
          "description": "What it does.", "author": "Someone", "section": "Tweaks",
          "depends": ["mobilesubstrate"],
          "layout": [{"path": "/Library/MobileSubstrate/DynamicLibraries/MyTweak.dylib", "product": "tweak"}],
          "tweakFilter": {"dylib": "tweak", "bundles": ["com.apple.springboard"]}
        }
        ```
        """,
        files: ["apelin-project.schema.json": MinisApelinProjectSchema.text],
    )
}

// Source: vendor/apelin/Packages/ApelinAgent/Sources/ApelinAgent/Skills/BuiltIn/TweakDevelopment.swift
extension MinisApelinBuiltInSkills {
    static let tweakDevelopment = MinisApelinSkillDocument(
        name: "apelin-tweak-development",
        description: "Writing and changing a tweak in an Apelin project: layout, hooks, filters, the on-device toolchain and SDK, and the edit-build loop.",
        body: """
        # Tweak development on the device

        You are on a jailbroken iPhone or iPad (iOS 16 or later, roothide or
        rootless). The project lives in `~/Documents/wiki.qaq.apelin/Projects/<name>/`
        and every file tool works relative to that root. Nothing you write can
        leave it.

        ## The project
        - `apelin-project.json` is the build description: sources, compile and
          link flags, frameworks, the deployment target (16.0) and build steps.
          Changing it, or a build script it names, makes the next `apelin_project_build` ask the
          user once (Strict mode); editing sources never does. Change it only
          when the task needs it, and say why. The apelin-project-description skill
          lists every member; read it before you edit the file.
        - Sources are Objective-C by default; write hooks the way the
          project's template does (its hook API comes from the injection
          framework the tweak extension depends on). Swift is available only if
          the user set it up with the toolchain.
        - The filter plist (`<Tweak>.plist`) selects the processes the dylib
          loads into, by bundle id (`Filter.Bundles`) or executable
          (`Filter.Executables`). SpringBoard is `com.apple.springboard`.
        - Read the template's files before writing new ones; keep its naming.
        - `apelin-icon.png`, beside `apelin-project.json`, is the project's
          icon in Apelin: a square PNG, 1024 points or less. Write it when the
          user asks for an icon; without it Apelin shows its kind's default.
          It is Apelin's alone and never goes into the package.
        - A path in code, a maintainer script or a plist is spelled as the
          apelin-bootstrap-paths skill says; logging is the apelin-tweak-runtime skill's.

        ## Finding the API you hook
        - `apelin_sdk_analyze_framework` lists a framework's exported symbols and
          Objective-C classes from the SDK's `.tbd`; `symbol_filter` narrows
          them. Both SDK tools look in `Frameworks`, then `PrivateFrameworks`,
          so `private` can be left out.
        - `apelin_sdk_read_header` reads a public header; with `symbol` in place
          of `header` it finds the header that declares a class, protocol, type
          or function. Private frameworks usually have no headers in the SDK:
          declare the interface you need in a local header, only the selectors
          you call.
        - Check availability: the build uses `-Werror=unguarded-availability-new`.
          Guard newer API with `@available(iOS 17.0, *)`.

        ## Writing hooks
        - Hook the narrowest method that produces the behaviour. Call the
          original unless the task is to replace it.
        - Never block the main thread in SpringBoard; a hang there is a
          respring loop for the user.
        - Keep the template's load-time marker log line intact so
          `apelin_package_confirm_loaded` can see it.
        - Symbols are hidden by default (`-fvisibility=hidden`); export only
          what the loader needs.

        ## Beside other tweaks
        Other tweaks load into the same process and often hook the same
        methods.
        - Install hooks with `MSHookMessageEx` or `MSHookFunction`, which
          chain with theirs, and call the original.
          `method_exchangeImplementations` and `method_setImplementation`
          can drop another tweak's hook.
        - Everything you name shares the process with them. Keep hook
          functions, saved originals and globals `static`. Give any method
          you add to a system class, and any class you create, your tweak's
          own prefix: when two tweaks use one name, one of them silently
          loses. Never override a system method in a category.
        - Key associated objects by the address of a `static` variable,
          never by a string.

        ## Theos and Logos
        Apelin does not build Theos projects: there is no Logos preprocessor
        and no Theos makefile system, and an Objective-C product compiles
        only `.m` and `.c` files (no `.x`, `.xm`, `.mm` or C++). Never
        download or vendor Theos. Rewrite a Theos tweak instead, keeping its
        logic, in the template's style:
        - A `%hook Class` method becomes a `static` function taking `id self,
          SEL _cmd` and the method's arguments, installed in the constructor
          with `MSHookMessageEx(objc_getClass("Class"), @selector(name:),
          (IMP)Hooked, (IMP *)&Original)`; `%orig` becomes
          `Original(self, _cmd, …)`. A class method hooks the metaclass,
          `object_getClass(objc_getClass("Class"))`.
        - `%ctor` is `__attribute__((constructor)) static void …(void)`;
          `%group` and `%init` are that constructor installing each group's
          hooks when its condition holds.
        - `%c(Name)` is `objc_getClass("Name")`; `%new` is `class_addMethod`
          with the method's type encoding; `%property` is a getter and setter
          on `objc_getAssociatedObject`; `%hookf` is `MSHookFunction`; `%log`
          is `os_log`.
        - Logos gave every hook a mangled name unique to the tweak
          (`_logos_method$…`); a rewrite names them itself, by the rules in
          Beside other tweaks. A Swift or C++ function is hooked by its
          mangled symbol, as apelin_sdk_analyze_framework lists it, looked up with
          `dlsym` or `MSFindSymbol`: a demangled name finds nothing. Check
          the symbol on each iOS version the package supports.
        - A `.x` file becomes `.m`; an `.xm` that uses C++ is rewritten
          without it.
        - The build links the SDK's public frameworks only. Reach a private
          class at run time with `objc_getClass`, and a private function with
          `dlopen` and `dlsym`.
        - Never link with `-undefined dynamic_lookup` or `suppress`, in
          `linkFlags` or a build script: CLT 15.0's linker then binds an
          arm64e slice without pointer authentication, and it crashes on its
          first constant string or imported call (the build refuses one).
          Leave a symbol to load time with `-Wl,-U,_symbol`, one per symbol.
        - A roothide-aware repository's `jbroot(…)` and a rootless one's
          `ROOT_PATH_NS(…)` become the apelin-bootstrap-paths skill's
          `ApelinBootstrapPath(…)`.
        - An imported repository stays a workspace: its Theos Makefile is
          replaced by a build script of its own that compiles the rewritten
          sources with the toolchain (the apelin-external-build skill), reading
          `TWEAK_NAME`, `_FILES`, `_FRAMEWORKS`, `_LIBRARIES`, `_CFLAGS`, the
          filter plist and `control` for what to build and package. Keep the
          package id, version and author the repository already has.

        ## The loop
        1. Read the relevant files (`file_read`, `file_grep`, `file_find`).
        2. Edit with `file_edit` (exact snippet, one occurrence) or
           `file_write`/`file_create` for new files.
        3. `apelin_project_build`. Fix every error diagnostic by file and line, rebuild.
           Treat warnings as work unless they are in the template.
        4. Install and confirm as the apelin-packaging-and-install skill describes.
        5. Check the behaviour itself, not only that it loaded: bring up what
           you changed, read it with `apelin_screen_read_tree`, drive it with
           `apelin_screen_interact`, and read it again after each action;
           while Ask Before Using the Screen is on, first tell the user what
           you will open, read and do there and why, and go on only once
           they consent explicitly (the prompt's rule, in every mode);
           behaviour that does not show in the UI tree is checked in what it
           logs (`apelin_log_read`), or in a screenshot only once the user
           has agreed to that one (the prompt's rule, in every mode). Report
           what you could not drive or see as Not tested.
        Keep each step its own call: build, install, respring and
        apelin_package_confirm_loaded are approved one by one.
        """,
    )
}

// Source: vendor/apelin/Packages/ApelinAgent/Sources/ApelinAgent/Skills/BuiltIn/TweakRuntime.swift
extension MinisApelinBuiltInSkills {
    static let tweakRuntime = MinisApelinSkillDocument(
        name: "apelin-tweak-runtime",
        description: "How a tweak behaves at run time inside another process: logging with os_log so apelin_log_read can see it, what the host's sandbox lets it read and write, reading settings, and telling another process something changed with a Darwin notification. Read when a tweak logs, stores a setting, reads files, or talks to another process.",
        body: """
        # A tweak at run time

        Your dylib runs inside the host process (an app, SpringBoard, a
        daemon), under that process's user, sandbox and entitlements. You
        cannot grant yourself more; design within what the host already has.
        Paths in anything below are spelled as the apelin-bootstrap-paths skill says.

        ## Logging — the only record you can read back
        - Log with `os_log` to a logger whose subsystem is the package ID, as
          the template's `TweakLog()` does
          (`os_log_create(APELIN_PACKAGE_ID, "<category>")`). This is how
          apelin_log_read and apelin_package_confirm_loaded find your lines.
        - The unified log has no history on iOS: it is read live, so a line is
          seen only while something listens. apelin_log_read listens from the
          moment it is called for at most 10 seconds (`listen_seconds` sets
          how long; nothing is read back), and your tool calls run one at a
          time, so it hears only what the host logs during the call on its
          own: something that recurs, or something the user does while it
          runs — tell them first. A line logged once at the host's launch is
          apelin_package_confirm_loaded's (with `respring: true` for SpringBoard). To trace
          a hook, log on each call, with the build ID, rate-limited if it is
          hot. Filter by `subsystem` (your package ID; dotted children
          included).
        - A value in a log line is private by default: integers, floats and
          bools print, but a string or object is redacted unless you mark it
          `%{public}`. Mark what you need to read, and never log a secret.
          Keep the `ApelinLoaded` marker public.
        - Levels are `debug`, `info`, `default`, `error`, `fault`; `default`
          and `notice` are one level to the reader. The system keeps `debug`
          and `info` only in memory, so log what you must read back with
          `os_log` (default) or `os_log_error`. A busy device drops lines the
          stream cannot hold.
        - Do not write a log file of your own inside the host: the host's
          sandbox usually forbids it, and a file in a place a sandboxed
          process cannot reach is lost. The unified log is the record; if a
          file is truly needed, it belongs to a daemon your package ships,
          not to the injected dylib.

        ## The sandbox you inherit
        - A tweak in a **sandboxed app** is confined to that app: it reads the
          app's own container and world-readable system files, and little
          else. Reading another app's container, the keychain or a data vault
          (`/var/mobile/Library/Photos`, `/var/mobile/Library/Preferences`
          from inside a sandbox, …) needs an entitlement the host does not
          have, so it fails with EPERM — root does not lift a data vault, and
          you are not root inside an app anyway.
        - Files a jailbroken process can read live under the bootstrap root.
          Of those, a tweak can reliably **write** only under the bootstrap's
          `/var` (apelin-bootstrap-paths). Do not try to write elsewhere from the
          injected code; if the task needs a privileged or shared write, a
          daemon your package ships does it.
        - Probe access by trying the operation (open the file, `opendir` the
          folder) and handling the error, not with `access()`: a sandboxed
          process can often `stat` a directory it cannot list.

        ## Reading a setting
        Two ways, both fine; pick one and be consistent.
        - **A plist you own**, read directly: a dictionary at
          `<bootstrap>/var/mobile/Library/Preferences/<package id>.plist`
          (make the path with apelin-bootstrap-paths). This avoids cfprefsd, which a
          sandboxed app calling it in a constructor has been seen to deadlock
          with, and whose cross-domain reads some jailbreaks refuse from a
          sandbox. The apelin-preference-bundle skill's Settings pane writes this
          file and posts a notification.
        - **CFPreferences** in your own domain
          (`CFPreferencesCopyAppValue(key, CFSTR(APELIN_PACKAGE_ID))`), read
          after the host is up, not in the constructor. cfprefsd flushes to
          disk a few seconds after a write, so a direct file read right after
          a CFPreferences write can see the old bytes.
        Fall back to a coded default for a missing key. Many hosts read a
        setting only at launch, so a change often takes effect on the host's
        next launch (a respring for SpringBoard).

        ## Telling another process something changed
        - A Darwin notification is the lightweight signal between processes:
          `notify_register_dispatch(name, &token, queue, handler)` to listen,
          `notify_post(name)` to fire. Name it under your package id, such as
          `<package id>/changed`, as the preference pane does.
        - It carries **no payload**: object, userInfo and "deliver
          immediately" are all ignored. It says only "this happened"; the
          receiver then re-reads the setting or file. Delivery needs the
          main run loop running in a common mode.
        - It is system-wide and has no sessions; post only names whose
          listeners you understand, and never a `com.apple.*` name. notifyd
          keeps a name's state only while something is registered for it.
        - For a value, not a signal, write the plist (or a file) and post the
          notification; the reader reads the file. A file two processes share
          is replaced atomically (write a temporary beside it, `rename`),
          which changes the inode, so a watcher watches the **directory**, not
          the file.
        """,
    )
}
